00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:32.720
When looking for mobile vulnerabilities with big&nbsp;
impact, one of things you need to check is how the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:40.880
application uses custom URIs. On android, there&nbsp;
are URIs with content:// protocol. They allow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:49.040
reading one application's file or data from the&nbsp;
other one. An example of such URI looks like this:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:56.960
The first part is called content provider and&nbsp;
it indicates what application will be used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:04.960
to handle this URI. In this case, it will be&nbsp;
mobile Firefox browser. And the second part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:12.560
is the path of a file that will be opened. And&nbsp;
as you saw on the example, mobile Firefox browser&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:19.520
allowed reading local files using those&nbsp;
content URIs. But after opening such address,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:27.440
Firefox copied contents of this file into a&nbsp;
temporary location and redirected user into this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:35.040
location, but not with the content:// protocol but&nbsp;
with a file:// protocol. It's simply used to read&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:42.720
local files. And what's important, the name of the&nbsp;
file in the temporary location remained unchanged.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:51.680
When defining those content URIs, the application&nbsp;
can specify directories that a user can open.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:55.360
But Firefox used the following configuration.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:02.080
It means that the user can read any&nbsp;
file that Firefox has access to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:10.800
And among other configuration files inside Firefox&nbsp;
directory, there is one that will be especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:17.840
interesting for the attacker. It's cookies.sqlite&nbsp;
which contains all cookies for a specific profile.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:25.520
Stealing those files would allow the attacker&nbsp;
to take over all the accounts to which he or she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:32.960
is currently logged in. But first, the attacker&nbsp;
would have to read profiles.ini file which would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:39.680
reveal the locations of cookie databases.&nbsp;
So our goal will be stealing those files&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:46.160
and the most likely attack scenario will be&nbsp;
that the victim visits our malicious website&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:55.040
that contains the exploit. It's easily possible&nbsp;
to create a website that opens a local file&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.040 --> 00:03:01.840
and iframes its content but still the parent&nbsp;
website has no access to those information.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:11.200
It's because Same-Origin-Policy would block&nbsp;
such attempts. It's a browser security mechanism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:16.560
that protects the websites from different&nbsp;
domains to steal each other's data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:24.880
In general, websites need to share the same domain&nbsp;
in order to be able to access each other's data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:32.800
But the question is how do sites with file://&nbsp;
protocol obey Same-Origin-Policy because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:40.240
do not have any domains. Turns out browsers are&nbsp;
very strict about this and it's really hard to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:48.080
read others file contents. But i'd like to&nbsp;
point out the obvious fact that a file can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:55.680
access its own contents. Or being more precise,&nbsp;
contents of a file stored under the same path.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:02.880
And the browser wouldn't notice if under the&nbsp;
same path would be a completely different file.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:11.440
And coming back to Firefox, all files are being&nbsp;
copied into the same directory and their names are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:18.480
not being changed, so it's possible to have two&nbsp;
files in different directories but with the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:26.080
name and once they are copied into the contentIri&nbsp;
directory, the file will be overwritten.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:36.000
And that simple idea was worth $5,000 here. Let's&nbsp;
see how the attack worked&nbsp;and explain the exploit.

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:42.160
So we create a&nbsp;website and we&nbsp;
assume that a victim visits it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:48.800
This is its source code. Our goal at the&nbsp;
moment is stealing the profiles.ini file&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:56.880
from Firefox directory. The iframe will be&nbsp;
executed first. It will download our file&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:04.000
with the name profiles.ini onto the SD card. We'll&nbsp;
take a look at contents of this file in a second.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:11.680
Then, android intent is being used and&nbsp;
without getting into details, it's the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:18.320
as we would use this content:// URI but&nbsp;
with additionally stated html content type.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:25.520
It would tell the browser to execute contents&nbsp;
of this file even though it has .ini extension.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:32.800
This will lead into Firefox trying to open&nbsp;
our downloaded file with a content:// URI.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:41.840
And as you already know, first, Firefox will copy&nbsp;
this file into contentUri directory and it will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:49.520
opened in the browser using file:// protocol.&nbsp;
Let's now see its contents. First we open an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:56.640
iframe with content:// URI which points to the&nbsp;
actual profiles.ini file that we want to steal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:06.240
Then, Firefox copies this file into contentUri&nbsp;
directory, overwriting our profiles.ini file

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:14.080
but the old version is still being opened in the&nbsp;
browser. Then, using javascript, we send a request&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:22.640
to window.location. It's a property that contains&nbsp;
information about the current address. So it looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:29.520
like the file would just get its own contents&nbsp;
which is of course allowed by Same-Origin-Policy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:36.480
but at the moment of this request, the file is&nbsp;
no longer the same because it was overwritten.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:44.400
So we can access contents of this file and&nbsp;
easily send it to our server, achieving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:52.160
our goal of stealing the profiles.ini file. It&nbsp;
would reveal the locations of cookie databases and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:59.840
we could steal those files exactly the same way.&nbsp;
The whole process could be automated so that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:08.320
exploit does not require any more user interaction&nbsp;
than visiting the website. As mentioned before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:14.960
the researcher was awarded $5,000 for this&nbsp;
vulnerability. It was fixed shortly after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:21.840
being reported to the program and this exploit no&nbsp;
longer works. I think the way the vulnerability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:29.200
was exploited was really creative and oddly&nbsp;
satisfying to me. If you feel like you've learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

